```text
Major trauma + suspected haemorrhagic shock
                |
                v
Control external bleeding + pelvic binder/tourniquet + warm patient
                |
                v
Activate MTP --> balanced blood products, minimal crystalloid
                |
                v
TXA if <3 h + serial ionized calcium + active rewarming
                |
                v
Identify source of bleeding
     |                 |                 |
     v                 v                 v
Chest             Abdomen            Pelvis/limb
OR/EDT selected    Laparotomy         Packing/IR/tourniquet
                |
                v
Physiologically exhausted?
(acidosis, hypothermia, coagulopathy, ongoing transfusion)
                |
         Yes ---+--- No
          |            |
          v            v
DCS: pack,          Definitive
ligate/shunt,       repair if safe
control contamination,
temporary closure
          |
          v
ICU correction of physiology
          |
          v
Planned relook --> definitive reconstruction and closure
```


## Topic Pearls

- **DCR = haemorrhage control + haemostatic resuscitation + prevention/reversal of the lethal triad and hypocalcaemia.**
- **DCS = abbreviated surgery for bleeding and contamination control; definitive anatomical repair is deferred.**
- Use **minimal crystalloid**, early blood products, active warming, calcium monitoring/replacement, and prompt source control.
- **Permissive hypotension is inappropriate in TBI/spinal cord injury**, where cerebral/spinal perfusion must be protected.
- Give **TXA early and within 3 hours**: 1 g IV over 10 minutes, then 1 g over 8 hours. [^1_7]
- Give fibrinogen replacement for major bleeding with functional fibrinogen deficit or fibrinogen at or below 1.5 g/L. [^1_7]
- Choose DCS before physiological collapse; in the ICU, persistent shock after surgery means **ongoing bleeding until proven otherwise**.
